paragraph start
name: paragraph-tp-what-one
A track is an ordered walk through marked blocks of the book. It
is for anything done again and again or step by step: a daily
routine, an hourly care loop, a workout, learning a skill, a test
run, a checklist before a trip.
contract start
id: q1deb06
question: Is this one idea a reader could restate?
contract end
answer start
for: q1deb06
asked: Is this one idea a reader could restate?

answer end
paragraph end

paragraph start
name: paragraph-tp-what-goal
The goal: mark something once, then the ecosystem turns the marks
into a living routine. It tells you what to do now, remembers
what you did and grows with you through levels.
contract start
id: q1deb06
question: Is this one idea a reader could restate?
contract end
answer start
for: q1deb06
asked: Is this one idea a reader could restate?

answer end
paragraph end

paragraph start
name: paragraph-tp-what-name
Name: track. One word used everywhere, because routine is too
narrow: a track can also teach, test or prepare. The name is
cheap to change now and costly after the first block type exists,
so it is decided first.
contract start
id: q1deb06
question: Is this one idea a reader could restate?
contract end
answer start
for: q1deb06
asked: Is this one idea a reader could restate?

answer end
paragraph end

paragraph start
name: paragraph-tp-what-promises
Five promises. One: the original files never change and never
know which tracks use them. Two: everything computed stays out of
the page. Three: colors come only from the theme engine. Four:
every layer works alone, so the first layers are useful before
the last ones exist. Five: phone first, one hand, short taps.
contract start
id: q1deb06
question: Is this one idea a reader could restate?
contract end
answer start
